<!-- CONFIGURATION -->
<section class="lib-section" aria-labelledby="config-title">
  <div class="lib-wrap">
    <div class="section-label">Configuration</div>
    <h2 id="config-title">File plus environment overrides</h2>
    <p>Resolution order is built-in defaults, then <code>~/.mcp-librarian/config.json</code> (or the selected home), then supported environment variables. The wiki lists every key and the fields that have no environment equivalent.</p>
    <div class="lib-table-wrap" role="region" aria-label="Configuration variables">
      <table class="lib-table">
        <thead>
          <tr>
            <th scope="col">Variable</th>
            <th scope="col">Default</th>
            <th scope="col">Description</th>
          </tr>
        </thead>
        <tbody>
          <tr>
            <td>MCP_LIBRARIAN_HOME</td>
            <td><code>~/.mcp-librarian</code></td>
            <td>Base directory for <code>config.json</code>, <code>manifest.json</code>, flat skills, keys, and exports. The BM25 index is memory-only.</td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <td>MCP_LIBRARIAN_LOG_LEVEL</td>
            <td><code>info</code></td>
            <td>MCP notification threshold: <code>debug</code>, <code>info</code>, <code>warning</code>, or <code>error</code>. Logs are not written to a local log directory.</td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <td>MCP_LIBRARIAN_RATE_LIMIT</td>
            <td><code>200</code></td>
            <td>Maximum non-lifecycle MCP requests in a sliding 60-second window. An invalid or zero environment value falls back to 200.</td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <td>MCP_LIBRARIAN_SKILLS_REPO</td>
            <td><code>penumbraforge/mcp-librarian-skills</code></td>
            <td>GitHub repository used as the indexed pack source for <code>install_pack</code>.</td>
          </tr>
        </tbody>
      </table>
    </div>
  </div>
</section>

<!-- LOCAL MODELS -->
<section class="lib-section" aria-labelledby="local-title">
  <div class="lib-wrap">
    <div class="section-label">Local Models</div>
    <h2 id="local-title">Model-agnostic</h2>
    <p>mcp-librarian is a stdio MCP server, not a model host and not a direct Ollama, LM Studio, or llama.cpp integration. A separate MCP-capable client must connect the server to whichever model provider it uses.</p>
    <div class="lib-features-grid" role="list" style="margin-top: 24px;">
      <div class="lib-feature-card" role="listitem">
        <h3>MCP Clients</h3>
        <p>The installer has configuration paths for Claude Code, Cursor, and Windsurf. That is an installer capability, not a claim that every version or configuration of those clients has been tested.</p>
      </div>
      <div class="lib-feature-card" role="listitem">
        <h3>Local Models</h3>
        <p>A fully local client can connect a local model to the server. Local search and loads stay on disk, while pack browsing/install and research still use the network. The model or client chooses tools and must handle their returned status fields correctly.</p>
      </div>
    </div>
    <p style="margin-top: 16px; font-size: 12px; color: var(--text-3); line-height: 1.6;">The server advertises the same 14 tool schemas to every MCP client. Actual tool selection, argument construction, review of untrusted research, and enforcement of <code>TAMPERED</code> status depend on the client and model. No model-size or quantization quality benchmark is claimed.</p>
  </div>
</section>

<!-- SECURITY -->
<section class="lib-section" aria-labelledby="security-title">
  <div class="lib-wrap">
    <div class="section-label">Security</div>
    <h2 id="security-title">Security model</h2>
    <p>mcp-librarian is written for the threat model of an AI agent with file system access, and assumes the agent can be manipulated through prompt injection in the knowledge base itself. These are pattern- and policy-based checks, not proofs: they will miss things and will occasionally flag content that is fine. No third-party audit has been done.</p>
    <ul class="lib-security-list" role="list">
      <li>
        <span class="lib-security-icon" aria-hidden="true">&#10003;</span>
        <span class="lib-security-text"><strong>Ed25519 status</strong> -- with a private key present, supported write paths re-sign the flat library, and indexing can label changed content <code>TAMPERED</code>. Load responses currently do not block or reliably attach that status, so clients must query and enforce it separately.</span>
      </li>
      <li>
        <span class="lib-security-icon" aria-hidden="true">&#10003;</span>
        <span class="lib-security-text"><strong>Content guard</strong> -- checks a short, published set of regex patterns on <code>create_skill</code> and each pack file. Pack files that match are individually rejected; accepted siblings may still install. Fenced and indented code regions are exempt, and edits made directly on disk bypass this check.</span>
      </li>
      <li>
        <span class="lib-security-icon" aria-hidden="true">&#10003;</span>
        <span class="lib-security-text"><strong>Path guard</strong> -- supported skill writes and export directories validate containment and reject traversal or symlink escape. This is a filesystem boundary for those code paths, not a sandbox around the MCP client or model.</span>
      </li>
      <li>
        <span class="lib-security-icon" aria-hidden="true">&#10003;</span>
        <span class="lib-security-text"><strong>Visible network surface</strong> -- <code>browse_packs</code>, <code>install_pack</code>, <code>research_topic</code>, and <code>fetch_page</code> are dedicated network tools; opt-in auto-research makes <code>find_and_load</code> conditionally networked. The current entrypoint has no telemetry or startup version-check request.</span>
      </li>
    </ul>
  </div>
</section>
